<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nostradamus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1566">1566</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Michel de Nostredame</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nostradamus, the Latinised name of Michel de Nostredame, was a French astrologer, apothecary and physician, born in Saint-Rémy-de-Provence to a family of Jewish origin. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and Montpellier, worked as an apothecary, fought outbreaks of plague and in 1547 settled in Salon-de-Provence with his second wife, Anne Ponsarde. After the success of his almanac for 1550 he worked as an astrologer for wealthy patrons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a> among them, and in 1555 published Les Prophéties, 942 quatrains said to predict future events. Academic sources reject any real prophetic power, judging his predictions vague.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nostradamus.jpg" alt="Nostradamus" width="330" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: César de Notre-Dame · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nostradamus_by_Cesar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nostradamus was one of at least nine children of the notary Jaume de Nostredame and Reynière, a granddaughter of Pierre de Saint-Rémy. His paternal family had been Jewish; his grandfather Cresquas, a grain and money dealer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> around 1459–60 and took the surname Nostredame. At fourteen he entered the University of Avignon, but left after a little more than a year when the university closed because of plague. He says he spent eight years from 1521 travelling the countryside to research herbal remedies. In 1529 he entered the University of Montpellier to study medicine but was expelled when it was found that he had been an apothecary, a manual trade the statutes banned, and that he had slandered doctors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1531 Jules-César Scaliger invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agen">Agen</a>, where he married; his wife and two children died in 1534, presumably of plague. In 1545 he helped the physician Louis Serre against an outbreak in Marseille and then dealt with others in Salon-de-Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a>. He wrote an almanac for 1550, the first time he printed the name Nostradamus, and its success led him to write one or more each year. His clients, including nobles from far away, asked for horoscopes, which he often made from birth charts they supplied. Professional astrologers such as Laurens Videl criticised him for incompetence.</p><p>The Prophecies, a book of mainly French quatrains, is grouped in sets of 100 quatrains called centuries. He obscured his meaning with Virgilianised syntax, word games and words from Greek, Italian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Provençal. Research suggests that much of the work paraphrases ancient end-of-the-world prophecies and passages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and medieval chroniclers. Most quatrains deal with undated disasters, and an underlying theme is an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by Muslim forces led by the expected Antichrist, reflecting the Ottoman advances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1566 his gout had turned into edema. In late June he drew up a will, and on 1 July he is said to have told his secretary Jean de Chavigny that he would not be alive at sunrise. He was reportedly found dead the next morning. He was buried in a Franciscan chapel in Salon and re-interred during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> in the Collégiale Saint-Laurent. Supporters credit him with predicting events such as the Great Fire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and the French Revolution, but no two interpretations agree. From the 1980s, the publication of his correspondence and of the original editions of 1555 and 1557 led scholars, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, to show that most claims lacked contemporary evidence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nostradamus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Caodaists hold that Victor Hugo, Sun Yat-sen and Trạng Trình, the Vietnamese <b>Nostradamus</b>, are the three main prophets of the Cao Đài pantheon, as a plaque at the entrance of the Tây Ninh Temple shows.</blockquote><a href="/religion/caodaism">Caodaism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
